WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.470
Tourism season in Florida,

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:04.671
Mexico and the Caribbean

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:06.740
is facing a huge threat.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.476
A gigantic blob of smelly seaweed

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.011
floating in the Atlantic,

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:12.379
and it's washing up on beaches

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:13.413
in the region.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.848
Leila Santiago shows us

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.082
how the Florida shores

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:19.619
are already being impacted.

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:20.920
It's thick

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.188
in the summertime,

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:24.491
builds up and smells terrible.

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:25.759
Joe Kaplan captured

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:27.360
these images about a week ago.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.229
Massive amounts of seaweed

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:30.430
washing up at Smathers

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.531
Beach, a beach he knows

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:32.999
well because he walks it

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:34.300
several times a week.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.301
I was shocked

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.803
when I saw it that day

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:38.972
where it wasn't even spring yet.

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:42.509
It's still winter, which is very unusual.

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:44.644
And this is about a 5000 miles long.

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:45.812
Professor Schulman,

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:47.447
who is one of the leading experts

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:48.982
on what many have referred to

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:50.784
as a massive blob of seaweed

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:52.719
heading to Florida's coast.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.921
Fair to call it a blob?

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.724
No, no, we can't call it a blob.

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.592
I would never call that a blob.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.094
Okay. Why?

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:02.495
Because he's not.

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:03.563
Satellite images,

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:04.531
he says show

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:07.033
it's not one massive body of seaweed.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:08.768
Rather, a bunch of patchy clumps

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.670
traveling from West Africa.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:11.171
It's called

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:13.173
the Atlantic Sargassum Belt

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.508
and is considered a natural phenomenon.

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:17.010
Right now, it's twice

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.211
the width of the U.S.

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:20.580
carrying 6 million tons of seaweed

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.650
and headed to the East Coast in June

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:24.484
of this year.

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:26.653
It may turn into $20 million.

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:27.954
So let me get this straight.

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:29.823
This what we're seeing the last month

00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:31.124
is 6 million tons.

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.093
And it's going to get bigger. Yes.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:34.594
There's no way to stop that.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:35.395
This is a nightmare.

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:35.862
It's just like

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:37.430
no one can stop a hurricane.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.965
Should we be worried about that? Nope.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:40.867
The reason

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.635
is the Sarkozys are not toxic,

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:44.137
but it smells pretty bad.

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:45.071
And it's a nuisance

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:46.573
for those trying to keep beaches

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:48.274
clean to attract tourists.

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:49.542
Just a few years ago,

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.444
here's what it looked like in Mexico.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:52.846
Officials in Monroe County,

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:53.580
which includes

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.315
the Florida Keys, have set aside

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:57.150
more than $200,000

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.751
to clean and remove Sargassum

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:00.053
from its beaches.

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:02.956
Seaweed is a mixed blessing.

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:03.790
We need it.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.892
Seaweed is a nursery

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:07.660
for all these large pelagic fish.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.362
The negative side to that seaweed is

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.030
if it comes in the concentrations

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.500
that I believe we're going to see,

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.367
our fishing grounds

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:14.701
are going to be

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:15.735
completely covered with it.

00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:17.003
There's almost no point to fishing

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:17.604
because we're going to be

00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:18.538
spending the entire day

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:20.440
cleaning weed off our lines.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.041
And as the Sargassum belt heads

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:23.710
toward Florida,

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.245
another natural phenomenon

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:26.112
is already hitting

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:27.714
its beaches on the West Coast.

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:29.115
Red tide

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:31.017
that can be toxic, kill fish

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.886
and cause respiratory issues.

00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:33.853
This year's red tide

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:35.155
concerns were enough to cancel

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:35.788
at least one

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.490
major event here in Indian rocks,

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:38.992
where one family visiting

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:39.926
told us

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:41.060
as soon as my son,

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.962
my husband and I got out of our car,

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:44.230
we all started coughing.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.231
Look for spring breakers

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:46.733
like this group from Iowa.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:47.300
The concerns

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.569
of massive amounts of seaweed or red tide

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:52.038
were not enough to change vacation plans.

00:02:52.272 --> 00:02:53.840
I would rather it be red tide

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.408
than raining every day.

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:58.344
Brian Lapointe is a research professor

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:59.612
at Florida Atlantic

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.581
University's Harbor Branch

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:03.683
Oceanographic Institute.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:04.784
And he joins me now

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:07.420
from Fort Pierce in Florida.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.555
Appreciate you being with us.

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:13.593
Well, thank you for the invite.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:16.796
So a massive blob of seaweed

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:18.331
spanning 5000 miles

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:19.933
is heading straight for the Florida

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:20.600
Gulf Coast.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.968
So vast, in fact,

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:24.170
that it can be seen from space.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.406
When do you expect it to hit the coast

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:28.608
and what might this signal?

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:30.276
The critical spring break

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:33.713
and of course, the summer months ahead

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:35.815
Well, this

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:38.618
the so-called blob of seaweed

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:39.852
is this seaweed

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:41.988
floating sargassum

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:43.990
in the tropical Atlantic Ocean.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:46.593
And that is what we call

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:49.262
the great Atlantic Sargassum belt.

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:52.999
And that that Sargassum

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:56.436
seaweed is moving towards the west

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:57.870
and will proceed

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.505
to move through the Caribbean

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.674
and up into the Gulf of Mexico

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:03.409
in South Florida area

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:05.511
in the coming months.

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:09.182
And we typically get the peak of

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:12.385
that seaweed arriving in Florida

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:15.188
around mid-summer.

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:17.490
July is usually when it peaks.

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:19.892
Yeah, I think I've been there

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.194
when it's arrived.

00:04:21.194 --> 00:04:25.064
So so why we seeing such a massive blob

00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:26.633
of seaweed at this time?

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:28.034
What's causing it?

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:29.469
And how big a threat

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:32.338
as opposed to residents and to sea life

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:37.477
Well, this is an unusual year

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:41.014
in that this Sargassum Bloom

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:43.716
in the great Atlantic Sargassum Belt

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:45.652
started early.

00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:47.153
It doubled in size

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:49.322
between December and January

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.792
in one month and doubled in size

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.228
and was larger in January

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:56.095
than it has ever

00:04:56.095 --> 00:05:00.066
been since this new region of Sargassum

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:02.402
growth began in 2011.

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:03.369
So this is

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:06.372
an entirely new oceanographic phenomenon.

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.475
Previously, the Sargasso was

00:05:09.776 --> 00:05:12.312
in the Sargasso Sea to the north,

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:14.781
that is the central gyre of the North

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:16.049
Atlantic Ocean

00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:17.083
and was circulate

00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:18.785
in the currents around that

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:20.820
as well as through the Caribbean

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.622
and the Gulf of Mexico.

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:24.624
So this is a whole new thing

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:27.927
and this is the source of this

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:30.196
Sargassum seaweed

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:33.199
that is is creating such a problem,

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:35.968
really a catastrophic problem

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.770
for for tourism

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.272
in the Caribbean region

00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:42.475
where it piles up on beaches up to five

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:45.111
or six feet deep on the beaches

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:46.112
in some places.

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:47.046
In fact,

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:49.949
in Barbados just last week, they reported

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:54.887
they need 1600 dump trucks a day

00:05:55.221 --> 00:05:57.423
to clean the beaches of this seaweed

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.560
to make it suitable for poor tourists.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.196
And recreation on the beaches.

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:05.264
And you know where it comes

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:07.600
ashore in the mangroves.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.671
It it comes in such large quantities

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:14.340
that it basically sucks the oxygen

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.141
out of the water

00:06:15.141 --> 00:06:15.708
and creates

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:18.044
what we refer to as dead zones.

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:20.113
These are normally

00:06:20.113 --> 00:06:22.882
a nursery habitats for fisheries.

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:27.186
And once once they're devoid of oxygen,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.390
we have lost that habitat

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:33.292
They are also harmful to seagrasses

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:34.894
and coral reefs

00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:38.464
and obviously to human health as well,

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:40.133
because when they rot

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:43.035
they release toxic hydrogen sulfide gas.

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:46.005
They have a high concentration of arsenic

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:47.373
in the tissue.

00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:49.075
So you have to be very careful

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:50.943
when you clean the beaches

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:52.445
and try to reuse this

00:06:52.445 --> 00:06:53.646
for fertilizers

00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:56.115
or some other beneficial use.

00:06:56.382 --> 00:06:58.918
So what solutions are available

00:06:59.352 --> 00:07:02.555
to stop these massive seaweed invasions?

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:04.490
Is there anything that can be done

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:05.291
to disperse

00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:06.192
or break it up,

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:07.860
or do we just need to do

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.095
what you've suggested

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:10.563
basically taking away

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:12.265
truckloads of this seaweed

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.034
Well,

00:07:15.034 --> 00:07:17.537
at this point, that's all we can do.

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.506
Efforts are being made in the Caribbean

00:07:20.973 --> 00:07:23.009
to construct booms,

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:25.244
to hold it off the beach

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:28.314
and to get harvesting boats

00:07:28.314 --> 00:07:29.849
to run along those booms

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:32.118
and harvest it into barges

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:34.320
where then it could be taken either

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.290
to land to a suitable disposal site

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:38.591
and dried

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:41.427
and perhaps used for some purpose or

00:07:42.395 --> 00:07:45.031
a new project is looking at actually

00:07:45.198 --> 00:07:45.965
harvesting it

00:07:45.965 --> 00:07:47.200
at sea

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.234
and sinking it

00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:50.102
to the bottom of the ocean.

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:52.705
And in that way, sequestering carbon

00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:54.440
that is, you know,

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:57.643
used by photosynthesis in the seaweed,

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.012
sending it to the bottom of the sea

00:08:00.012 --> 00:08:01.848
where it will be buried.

00:08:01.848 --> 00:08:04.750
And in that way, sequester that carbon

00:08:05.218 --> 00:08:06.752
to kind of chip away

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:09.055
at the CO2 build up in the atmosphere.

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:10.556
Bryan Lapointe,

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:12.158
thank you so much for joining us.

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:14.627
Appreciate it. You're welcome. Thank you.

